ings, or fundraising figures exist yet", "Term-limited incumbent removes any incumbency effect for either side", "2028 national environment and nominees remain unknown; headlines are primary speculation"], "rationale": "With no polls, ratings, or money figures to weigh, the forecast rests on New Jersey's four-point Democratic lean; the term-limited open seat adds no incumbency adjustment, so the expected margin stays near the state's lean with unusually wide uncertainty.", "watch": "Once both parties settle on nominees and the first New Jersey general-election polls arrive, a polarizing pick or a strong national wave for either side could move the margin several points.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "New Jersey enters the 2028 presidential race as a modest Democratic-leaning state, with a partisan lean of about four points toward Democrats. The incumbent president is term-limited, so neither party benefits from incumbency, and no general-election polls, expert ratings, or fundraising figures are available yet. Headline coverage so far focuses on possible contenders, including Chris Christie weighing a third bid and speculation around Kamala Harris, Gavin Newsom, and other Democrats, but none of it speaks directly to how New Jersey's general election will break.\n\nWith no polling to lean on, the expected margin sits near the state's four-point Democratic tilt, and uncertainty is wide because the national environment, both nominees, and the polling record are all unsettled. A strong or weak national cycle for either party could move New Jersey several points in either direction.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6761, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.642, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25",